'''Cuiba''' or '''Cuiva''' is a [[Guahiban languages|Guahiban]] language that is spoken by about 2,300 people in [[Colombia]] and additional 650 in [[Venezuela]].  More than half of Cuiba speakers are monolingual, and in Colombia there is a 45% literacy rate.{{Citation needed|date=May 2016}}<ref>{{Cite news |title=Cuiba Language (CUI) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use {{!}} Ethnologue Free |url=https://www.ethnologue.com/language/cui/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251210052145/https://www.ethnologue.com/language/cui/ |archive-date=2025-12-10 |access-date=2025-12-29 |work=Ethnologue (Free All) |language=en-US}}</ref> Cuiva is also referred to as '''Cuiba''', '''Cuiba-Wámonae''', '''Kuiva''', '''Chiricoa''', '''Hiwi''', and '''Maiben'''. In Colombia, Cuiva is spoken among those who live and who are born surrounding the Colombian rivers, [[Casanare River|Meta Casanare]] and [[Capanaparo River|Capanaparo]]. The [[Cuiba ethnic group]] is often found in the [[Casanare Department]]. In Venezuela the language is spoken in the state of [[Apure]], one of the states that border with Colombia, and which is found alongside the Capanaparo river.

